A||||8||ginoferrari||fan switch bypass||||9||
Z||000000||ginoferrari||09-19-2001||10:25 PM||||Has anyone out there bypassed their fan switch. I've been autocrossing and the car gets warn fast. My car's fan doesn't come on until it gets pretty hot. I'd like to install a switch so that I could turn on the fan manualy if that's posible. <P>------------------<BR>                  <IMG SRC="http://www.imagestation.com/picture/sraid26/p23177a738ceb9d88e0b51db344f1ee79/fe3c196f.jpg">                       <BR>84 2M4<BR>84 SE<BR>87 Coupe||||reg||9
Z||000001||FieroJoe||09-19-2001||10:33 PM||||If you have a one speed fan (small relay near left side headlight, 4 wires [I think]) you can do what I did.  I ran a splice from the big brown wire and the big red one.  The big red one is hot at all times, and the brown one is the feed for the fan.  I used another relay (bosch type) to connect these together whenever I throw a switch inside the car.  The switch connects the coil of the relay to ground, and the other side of the coil has +12V controlled by igntion, so I can't leave my fan on.  By using the relay, I didnt have to have 2 huge wires running into my pass. compartment and i could use a smaller switch.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||09-19-2001||10:36 PM||||There is an easier way. Just above the brake pedal is a junction block - tap into the green/wht stripe wire and ground it thru a switch- done||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mach10||09-19-2001||10:40 PM||||I did it, I just pulled the A/C control out, and splice-tapped into the GREEN/WHITE wire, and ran it to a switch, then ground. Works like a charm. Made a plate to fit where my ask-tray does. Nothing like flipping up the lid to hit a red push-button.<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000004||ginoferrari||09-19-2001||11:33 PM||||Thanks to you all, I will get that done before the next autocross on 10/14 and see how it works. This site is great for info on things, and you always get more then one way to do everything. Thanks again to all.||||reg||7
Z||000005||Captain Fireball||09-19-2001||11:53 PM||||Ok, so here's a different in correlence with the topic. My fan won't turn on at all. I've considered a bypass switch but I'm mainly concerned with finding out what's wrong. I've checked my relays and fusable links and thermostat and everything seems fine, but I can't seem to find any connection between the sensor on the engine and the fan relay. Can anyone tell me if the wire goes through the ECM at all first or does anyone have a wire diagram I could use?||||reg||9
Z||000006||Carrolles||09-20-2001||02:04 PM||||Wire diagrams can be found here:<BR> <A HREF="http://home.t-online.de/home/u.moeller-objects/schaltp.html" TARGET=_blank>http://home.t-online.de/home/u.moeller-objects/schaltp.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Phil||09-20-2001||07:31 PM||||Capt Fireball-- It helps greatly if you say what Fiero and engine you have - on 88 4 bangers the fan is computer controlled - on V6 it's a switch in the head||||reg||1
Z||000008||jimmybpei||09-20-2001||08:34 PM||||Ther is an eve easier way to get the fan to <BR>come on. <P>Go to the engine at the fan switch(should be a round push on connector) cut the green wire and ground it to a bolt in the block.<P>The fan will come on ass the as the ignition is on. have done this to all 3 of my cars after having problems before with over heating.<P>The car as well will not get even half as hot as it used to. <P>Will be doing this to my 4.5 caddy install just as an insurance. A new fan is cheaper than a 4.5 caddy motor<P>Hope this helps you out some.<P>Later<BR>Jimmy B.||||reg||1
